---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// Unlisted guide for prospective volunteers and apprentices. Not linked from the
// nav or footer; share the URL directly. Facts below come from the Virginia DWR
// Wildlife Rehabilitation Permit Conditions (revised 12/3/2025) and 4VAC15-30-50.
// Re-check against the current conditions PDF each January when permits renew.
const CONDITIONS_URL = 'https://dwr.virginia.gov/forms-download/wildlife-rehabilitator-conditions.pdf';
const APPLICATION_URL = 'https://dwr.virginia.gov/forms-download/wildlife-rehabilitator-application.pdf';
const REG_URL = 'https://law.lis.virginia.gov/admincode/title4/agency15/chapter30/section50/';
const CONDITIONS_DATE = 'December 3, 2025';
---

<Layout
  title="How to Get Permitted for Reptile and Amphibian Rehab in Virginia"
  description="A plain-language walkthrough of Virginia's wildlife rehabilitation permit categories for people who want to help native reptiles and amphibians with SERC."
  noindex={true}
>

  <div class="permit">

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="permit__header">
      <span class="permit__kicker">For prospective volunteers</span>
      <h1>Want to work with native reptiles and amphibians in Virginia? Here's how the permits work.</h1>
      <p class="permit__intro">
        The Virginia Department of Wildlife Resources permit conditions run about forty pages, and
        almost nobody reads, remembers, or absorbs all of them. This guide covers only what matters for
        reptiles and amphibians: box turtles, terrapins, rat snakes, mud turtles, tree frogs, that
        nest you found in your backyard. Read it once before you email us.
      </p>
      <p>
        Virginia issues wildlife rehabilitation permits in five categories:
      </p>
      <ul class="permit__cats">
        <li><a href="#cat-iv"><strong>Category IV</strong>, Care Provider</a>: hands-on care at a permitted facility, no animals at your home. Ages 16 and up (a legal custodian co-signs for minors)</li>
        <li><a href="#cat-i"><strong>Category I</strong>, Apprentice</a>: animals in your own home under a sponsoring rehabilitator. Ages 18 and up</li>
        <li><a href="#cat-ii"><strong>Category II</strong>, Independent rehabilitator</a>: the full permit, sick and injured animals from the public</li>
        <li><a href="#cat-iii"><strong>Category III</strong>, Wildlife hospital</a>: a professional veterinary facility, not an individual permit</li>
        <li><a href="#cat-v"><strong>Category V</strong>, Student or trainee</a>: 120 days of supervised training. Ages 16 and up (a legal custodian co-signs for minors)</li>
      </ul>
      <ol class="permit__nav" aria-label="Guide sections">
        <li><a href="#step-1" data-step-link="1">Before you apply</a></li>
        <li><a href="#step-2" data-step-link="2">The five permit levels</a></li>
        <li><a href="#step-3" data-step-link="3">Which level is for you</a></li>
        <li><a href="#step-4" data-step-link="4">What every permittee must do</a></li>
        <li><a href="#step-5" data-step-link="5">The reptile and amphibian part</a></li>
        <li><a href="#step-6" data-step-link="6">Next steps</a></li>
      </ol>
    </header>

    <!-- Step 1 -->
    <section class="permit__step" id="step-1" data-step="1">
      <div class="permit__step-label">Part 1 of 6</div>
      <h2>Before you apply: you might not need a permit at all</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Picking up and delivering a wild animal does not require a permit.</strong> Under
        Virginia regulation (<a href={REG_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">4VAC15-30-50</a>),
        anyone may take an injured, sick, or orphaned animal to a permitted rehabilitator or veterinarian,
        as long as you contact that individual first and they confirm they will take it. So if
        your instinct is to grab an injured turtle off the road and drive it to us, that is legal, that is
        helpful, and we would welcome that call.
      </p>
      <p>
        A permitted rehabilitator can also designate you as a
        volunteer transporter to move secured animals between rehabilitators,
        veterinarians, and release sites. If you love the driving part and never want to clean a
        turtle tub, this can be your whole job.
      </p>
      <p>
        The line in the sand: feeding, medicating, or housing a wild animal at your home for more
        than the time it takes to drive it to a rehabilitator requires a permit. That is what the
        rest of this guide is about.
      </p>
      <p class="permit__meta">
        Who can apply: no prior wildlife law convictions, anywhere, ever. Categories I to III need
        you to be 18 and a Virginia resident, though students at a Virginia college or university
        with a temporary Virginia address count as residents. Categories IV and V allow 16 and 17
        year olds with a legal custodian co-signing the application.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 2 -->
    <section class="permit__step" id="step-2" data-step="2">
      <div class="permit__step-label">Part 2 of 6</div>
      <h2>The five permit levels, in plain language</h2>
      <p>The levels stack. Most people start at Category IV or Category I.</p>

      <div class="permit__levels">
        <article class="permit__level" id="cat-iv">
          <div class="permit__level-name">Category IV</div>
          <div class="permit__level-title">Care Provider</div>
          <p>
            This is hands-on care at a permitted rehabilitator's facility, including when that
            rehabilitator is not there. Someone is coming down with the flu before a week of terrapin
            feedings? That is you. You do not keep animals at your own home, and you only work with
            the species the facility is permitted for.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>No sponsor needed and no facility inspection, because the animals never touch your house</li>
            <li>Can receive and hold the facility's animals, following its instructions, while transport is arranged, including when the regular permit holder is away</li>
            <li>Six hours of approved continuing education before you apply, then six hours every year</li>
            <li>After six months of experience, you can move to Category I with your sponsor's written recommendation</li>
            <li>Your time as a Category IV counts toward the two years of permitted experience needed for Category II</li>
            <li>A lesser-known path: two full years as a Category IV at the same facility lets you request a direct upgrade to Category II with a written recommendation from that facility's owner or director, skipping the apprentice step entirely</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="permit__level" id="cat-i">
          <div class="permit__level-name">Category I</div>
          <div class="permit__level-title">Apprentice</div>
          <p>
            You care for wildlife at your own
            home under a sponsoring Category II or III rehabilitator, and the rule says your cases
            stay "generally limited to uninjured or orphaned wildlife." For reptiles and amphibians,
            orphan covers eggs and hatchlings too: a nest dug up by a dog, eggs in a field about to
            be mowed, hatchlings that emerged in January with no chance outside. Those
            are the animals we send apprentices, and they are the heart of turtle rehab. An injured or hit-by-car turtle
            is not your patient to rehab, but she can spend the night in
            your spare tub: that is a bridge role, not a destination.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Requires a sponsor who commits to mentoring you for at least two years</li>
            <li>May receive, transport, and house animals outside your permit (even injured ones) for up to 24 hours while transport is arranged, with authorization and instructions given in advance by your sponsor, a cooperating veterinarian, or DWR</li>
            <li>DWR strongly encourages six months as a Category IV at your future sponsor's facility first</li>
            <li>Six hours of approved continuing education before you apply, then six hours every year</li>
            <li>Fifty hours per year of hands-on training with your sponsor, logged by date, topic, and hours</li>
            <li>Keep complete records of every animal on your permit: species, exact find location, who found it, treatment, outcome, and release date and place. We use WRMD and you will too. An animal list goes to DWR with your February 1 annual report</li>
            <li>A Conservation Police Officer inspects your home setup before the permit is issued</li>
            <li>You may not advertise to the public for animals; intakes come by referral from your sponsor, and you must notify your sponsor within 12 hours of receiving an animal</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="permit__level" id="cat-ii">
          <div class="permit__level-name">Category II</div>
          <div class="permit__level-title">Independent rehabilitator (IIA individual, IIB organization)</div>
          <p>
            The full permit. You take sick and injured wildlife, including hit-by-car snappers and
            dog-chewed cooters, directly from the public, in cooperation with a licensed
            veterinarian. This is the level SERC's rehabilitators hold, and it is what lets us
            accept sick and injured animals rather than only orphans.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Requires more than two years of permitted rehabilitation experience, counted from when you were first permitted. Time as a Category IV counts, not only time as a Category I</li>
            <li>Six hours of approved continuing education before you apply, then six hours every year</li>
            <li>Facility inspection, plus rabies vaccination or titer if you will work with rabies vector species (not a factor for reptiles and amphibians)</li>
            <li>Full patient records and a February 1 annual report to DWR for every animal, every year. For reptiles and amphibians the find-location field is the whole point: without it, the animal can never be released</li>
            <li>After two years at Category II, you can sponsor apprentices (up to five as an individual)</li>
            <li>Must be accessible to DWR and the public for consultation and assistance. In practice this means a working phone number, voicemail you check, and reasonable responsiveness, not a pager in the shower; line up backup when you travel</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="permit__level" id="cat-iii">
          <div class="permit__level-name">Category III</div>
          <div class="permit__level-title">Wildlife hospital (IIIA teaching hospital, IIIB hospital)</div>
          <p>
            A professionally run facility with veterinary equipment and a veterinarian on staff. This
            is the Wildlife Center of Virginia tier, not an individual permit. You will not apply for
            this.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="permit__level" id="cat-v">
          <div class="permit__level-name">Category V</div>
          <div class="permit__level-title">Student or trainee</div>
          <p>
            For veterinary students, other students, and out-of-state trainees who are passing
            through. Valid for 120 days, and you never touch an animal outside the same room as your
            supervisor. No prior training required. If you live in Virginia and actually want to do
            this, this is not your category: apply for Category IV instead.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 3 -->
    <section class="permit__step" id="step-3" data-step="3">
      <div class="permit__step-label">Part 3 of 6</div>
      <h2>Which level is for you</h2>
      <p>Find the row that sounds like you.</p>

      <div class="permit__table-wrap">
        <table class="permit__table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>If you want to...</th>
              <th>Apply for</th>
              <th>Why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Drive animals to us or to a vet, but not handle them</td>
              <td><strong>No permit</strong> (designated transporter)</td>
              <td>We can add you to our designated transporters. The animal rides in a carrier or ventilated box; you never touch them.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Do hands-on care at SERC's facility: feeding, soaking, cleaning, weighing, without keeping animals at home</td>
              <td><strong>Category IV</strong></td>
              <td>No sponsor, no home inspection, and it is the on-ramp DWR recommends before Category I.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Raise box turtle hatchlings, incubate rescued eggs, or overwinter recovering animals at your own home with our mentorship</td>
              <td><strong>Category I</strong></td>
              <td>The apprenticeship permit. Plan on six months as a Category IV with us first. The two-year clock toward Category II runs from your first permit, so that time counts. Ask us about sponsorship.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Run your own intake line, take hit-by-car turtles straight from the public, and work with your own vet</td>
              <td><strong>Category II</strong></td>
              <td>Requires two years of permitted experience, with Category IV and Category I time both counting. This is where the path leads.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Get experience for a semester or a summer, or you live outside Virginia</td>
              <td><strong>Category V</strong></td>
              <td>120 days, directly supervised, no prerequisites.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="permit__callout permit__callout--plain">
        <strong>Our suggestion:</strong> start as a Category IV. It gets you working with animals
        fastest, it is the least paperwork, and every month counts: six months qualifies you for
        Category I if you want animals at home, and the same time counts toward the two years needed
        for Category II. Many people also just remain Category IV and enjoy the role as caretakers.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 4 -->
    <section class="permit__step" id="step-4" data-step="4">
      <div class="permit__step-label">Part 4 of 6</div>
      <h2>What you sign up for at every level</h2>
      <p>These apply to every permit holder, Category IV included. None of them are scary. All of them are audited.</p>
      <ul class="permit__list">
        <li>
          <strong>Six hours of continuing education, every year.</strong> Approved sources include IWRC
          and NWRA courses, wildlife rehabilitation conferences, state rehabilitator associations, state
          and federal agencies, and relevant college courses. A log of time spent training with a
          Category II or III rehabilitator also counts toward your first six hours. Keep your
          certificates; proof goes to DWR with your renewal, so have them together by December.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Renew every year.</strong> All wildlife rehabilitation permits expire at midnight on
          January 31, and DWR needs about 30 days to process a renewal. SERC submits renewals in
          December so there is time to spare, and we will ask you to have yours ready then too.
          Sponsors submit their apprentices' renewals as part of their own package.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Never charge a fee</strong> for rehabilitation, pickup, or transport. Donations are
          fine; fees are not. This is one rule that gets permits revoked.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep DWR current.</strong> Report any change of name, address, or phone number within
          seven days.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 5 -->
    <section class="permit__step" id="step-5" data-step="5">
      <div class="permit__step-label">Part 5 of 6</div>
      <h2>The part that is different for reptiles and amphibians</h2>
      <p>
        Most of the permit conditions were written with birds and mammals in mind. A few rules land
        very differently for our animals, and this is the section people wish they had read first.
      </p>
      <ul class="permit__list">
        <li>
          <strong>Release goes back to the point of collection.</strong> DWR requires reptiles and
          amphibians to be released where they were found, if that spot is safe habitat. If the location
          is unknown, the animal cannot be released at all. This is why the intake location is the most
          important field on every record we keep, and why we never publish it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>There is a release season.</strong> In central and eastern Virginia, an animal not
          ready by October 16 must be overwintered in captivity and released on or after May 1. (West of
          the Blue Ridge the cutoff is October 1.) Overwintering is a big part of what we do: a box
          turtle admitted in July with a bad shell may still be in your tub the following April. Reptile
          rehab is slow, and the animals that need us most are the ones that take the longest.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Housing has minimum standards.</strong> Appendix F of the conditions sets enclosure
          sizes and temperatures for reptiles: one animal per enclosure (turtles are not social, and
          company slows recovery), a proper heat gradient (box turtles need roughly 70 to 85°F with a
          basking spot around 88°F), warm water at 76°F for aquatic species like sliders and cooters,
          and species-appropriate humidity and substrate. If you pursue Category I, your home setup will
          be inspected against these.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No rabies requirements.</strong> The rabies vaccination and titer rules apply to
          rabies vector mammals. They do not apply to reptile and amphibian work, which quietly removes
          one of the more expensive hurdles that bird and mammal rehabbers face.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Step 6 -->
    <section class="permit__step" id="step-6" data-step="6">
      <div class="permit__step-label">Part 6 of 6</div>
      <h2>Next steps</h2>
      <ol class="permit__list permit__list--numbered">
        <li>
          <strong>Decide on a level</strong> using Part 3. If you are unsure, Category IV is the safe
          answer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Get your first six hours of continuing education.</strong> We will point you to
          approved options, and hours training with us count.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Talk to us.</strong> Email <a href="mailto:info@southeastreptile.org">info@southeastreptile.org</a>
          and tell us which level you are aiming for. We will send the step-by-step application
          instructions, the forms, and the training documentation you need. For Category I, this is
          also where we talk through sponsorship and what a home setup needs before the inspection.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Apply to DWR.</strong> Applications go to the DWR Permits Section with a
          non-refundable fee. Allow 30 days. Categories I and II include a home inspection by a
          Conservation Police Officer before the permit is issued.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="permit__meta">
        Source documents: the
        <a href={CONDITIONS_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Virginia Wildlife Rehabilitation Permit Conditions</a>
        (revised {CONDITIONS_DATE}) and the
        <a href={APPLICATION_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">permit application</a>. The conditions
        are the authority; if anything here conflicts with them, they win. Please tell us so we can fix it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Step controls (enhanced by the script below) -->
    <nav class="permit__controls" aria-label="Guide navigation" hidden>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--outline" data-step-prev>Back</button>
      <span class="permit__progress" data-step-progress></span>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn--dark" data-step-next>Next</button>
    </nav>

  </div>

  <style>
    .permit { max-width: 820px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 4rem var(--site-gutter) 5rem; }
    .permit__kicker {
      display: block; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: 0.14em;
      text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-text-faint); margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }
    .permit__header h1 { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
    .permit__intro { color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.8; max-width: 680px; margin-bottom: 2rem; }
    .permit__nav {
      list-style: none; margin: 0 0 1rem; padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
      background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
      display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
      counter-reset: steps;
    }
    .permit__nav li { counter-increment: steps; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.6; }
    .permit__nav li::before { content: counter(steps) ". "; color: var(--color-text-faint); }
    .permit__nav a { color: var(--color-text); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
    .permit__nav a[aria-current="step"] { font-weight: 600; text-decoration: none; }
    .permit__cats {
      margin: 0 0 1.25rem; padding-left: 1.25rem; max-width: 680px;
      display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.5rem;
    }
    .permit__cats li { font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-text-muted); }
    .permit__cats a { color: var(--color-text); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
    .permit__cats strong { font-weight: 500; }
    .permit__level:target { border-color: var(--color-text); }

    .permit__step { padding: 2.5rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .permit__step-label { font-size: 11px; font-weight: 500; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-text-faint); margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
    .permit__step h2 { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-weight: 300; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
    .permit__step p { color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.1rem; }
    .permit__step a { color: var(--color-text); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
    .permit__step strong { font-weight: 500; color: var(--color-text); }
    .permit__meta { font-size: 14px !important; color: var(--color-text-faint) !important; }

    .permit__callout {
      padding: 1.1rem 1.35rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 1.5rem;
      background: #fdf8f2; border: 1px solid #c8b898; border-left: 3px solid #a07840;
      font-size: 15px; color: var(--color-text); line-height: 1.75;
    }
    .permit__callout--plain { background: var(--color-surface); border-color: var(--color-border); border-left-color: var(--color-text); }

    .permit__levels { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
    .permit__level { background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); padding: 1.5rem 1.75rem; }
    .permit__level-name { font-size: 11px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-text-faint); margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
    .permit__level-title { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-size: 1.35rem; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
    .permit__level p { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; font-size: 15px; }
    .permit__level ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.4rem; }
    .permit__level li { font-size: 14px; color: var(--color-text-muted); line-height: 1.65; }

    .permit__table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 1.5rem 0; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .permit__table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 15px; min-width: 560px; }
    .permit__table th { text-align: left; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-text-faint); padding: 0.85rem 1rem; background: var(--color-surface); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .permit__table td { padding: 0.9rem 1rem; vertical-align: top; color: var(--color-text-muted); line-height: 1.6; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .permit__table tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }

    .permit__list { margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem; padding-left: 1.25rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.9rem; }
    .permit__list li { color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.75; }
    .permit__list--numbered { list-style: decimal; }

    .permit__controls { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; padding-top: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
    .permit__progress { font-size: 13px; color: var(--color-text-faint); letter-spacing: 0.06em; }

    /* Click-through mode (JS adds .permit--stepper): one part at a time. */
    .permit--stepper .permit__step { display: none; border-top: none; padding-top: 1.5rem; }
    .permit--stepper .permit__step.is-active { display: block; }
    .permit--stepper .permit__controls { display: flex; }
  </style>

  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      var root = document.querySelector('.permit');
      if (!root) return;
      var steps = Array.prototype.slice.call(root.querySelectorAll('[data-step]'));
      var links = Array.prototype.slice.call(root.querySelectorAll('[data-step-link]'));
      var controls = root.querySelector('.permit__controls');
      var prev = root.querySelector('[data-step-prev]');
      var next = root.querySelector('[data-step-next]');
      var progress = root.querySelector('[data-step-progress]');
      if (!steps.length || !controls) return;

      root.classList.add('permit--stepper');

      function catTarget() {
        var m = (location.hash || '').match(/^#(cat-[ivx]+)$/);
        return m ? document.getElementById(m[1]) : null;
      }

      // 0 means the overview: header only, no part open, no Back/Next bar.
      function fromHash() {
        var el = catTarget();
        if (el) {
          var host = el.closest('[data-step]');
          if (host) return Math.min(Math.max(steps.indexOf(host) + 1, 1), steps.length);
        }
        var m = (location.hash || '').match(/^#step-(\d+)$/);
        if (!m) return 0;
        return Math.min(Math.max(parseInt(m[1], 10), 1), steps.length);
      }

      function show(n, scroll) {
        steps.forEach(function (s, i) { s.classList.toggle('is-active', i === n - 1); });
        links.forEach(function (a, i) {
          if (i === n - 1) a.setAttribute('aria-current', 'step'); else a.removeAttribute('aria-current');
        });
        controls.hidden = n === 0;
        if (n === 0) {
          if (history.replaceState) history.replaceState(null, '', location.pathname);
          if (scroll) window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' });
          return;
        }
        prev.textContent = n === 1 ? 'Overview' : 'Back';
        next.textContent = n === steps.length ? 'Back to overview' : 'Next';
        progress.textContent = 'Part ' + n + ' of ' + steps.length;
        var cat = catTarget();
        if (history.replaceState) history.replaceState(null, '', '#step-' + n);
        if (cat) {
          cat.scrollIntoView({ behavior: scroll ? 'smooth' : 'auto', block: 'start' });
        } else if (scroll) {
          var top = root.querySelector('.permit__header').getBoundingClientRect().bottom + window.scrollY - 24;
          window.scrollTo({ top: top, behavior: 'smooth' });
        }
      }

      var current = fromHash();
      show(current, false);

      prev.addEventListener('click', function () { current = Math.max(current - 1, 0); show(current, true); });
      next.addEventListener('click', function () { current = current === steps.length ? 0 : current + 1; show(current, true); });
      links.forEach(function (a, i) {
        a.addEventListener('click', function (e) { e.preventDefault(); current = i + 1; show(current, true); });
      });
      window.addEventListener('hashchange', function () { current = fromHash(); show(current, true); });
    })();
  </script>

</Layout>
